dds a delivery guide of up to 4,000 characters to the system prompt and registers pre_gateway_dispatch (described next). With guide_mode: generate it also registers two agent tools. chip_status reads the plugin's settings, Hermes's tts configuration and this session's last authored register and reports them; it sends nothing and changes nothing. chip_preview renders a score offline and returns the text each engine would receive and the cues it would drop; no synthesis, no network, nothing written. While the chip is the speech provider, each successful text_to_speech result gains an emotion_chip entry of at most 600 characters (the dialect, the agent's cue words, drops and notes); it stays in the conversation and is sent nowhere. The pre_gateway_dispatch hook: for each incoming message it asks the gateway whether it will read the reply aloud and keeps that answer in memory under the chat and message id (at most 256, never used after 15 minutes), used once to add one line to that message's turn; it never skips or changes a message, and registering the hook routes webhook and relay deliveries through Hermes's hooked ingress path. When another speech provider is selected, its tool_request middleware rewrites text_to_speech text containing a bracket for that provider (the Fish dialect on Fish S2 and Drama 3 models, or the ElevenLabs dialect on a v3/v4 model; for any other provider or model, including Hermes's default Edge, Fish S1 and ElevenLabs on older models, standalone bracketed words are removed as cues, a bracketed Step 2 too, while the words inside spans, numeric references such as [1] and code such as an array index stay), leaves command providers alone and records nothing. With signals on (off by default) it adds a dated situation line of up to 200 characters to the turn and, when a tool call fails, to that call's result. It reads Hermes's tts configuration (read-only) to pick the dialect, model and voice. It writes under the profile's plugin-data/emotion-chip/ 