+version: "0.2.1"
+description: "Try Hermes without leaving Grok. Gives each Grok desktop-app bot a Hermes twin profile and a Desktop Twins page (conflicts, restore points); `hermes twin demo` needs no Grok account. Name and description changes reach the twin in seconds; instructions are pasted by you or imported from the setup export the bot writes about itself (routines arrive paused, connectors as names only, its memories are dropped); skills travel through optional guided skill sync (a sync bot in your own Grok account); a bot's Grok memory and model setting are not copied. Several new twins are finished in one sheet: one model for all of them (model servers already set up in Hermes listed first) and each twin's instructions from its own setup export, picked up from Downloads, a dropped file or skill sync's shared folder; optionally Hermes does the Grok part with computer use, asking before every step. Optional and off by default: the same twins on your other Hermes computers (macOS, Linux, Windows), linked once with a short code, with settings that follow by a fixed allowlist, skills, routines and end-to-end-encrypted memory each behind its own switch, and requests from one of your computers to a twin on another. Unofficial; not affiliated with xAI. Disclosure — Runs the user-installed external botport CLI (never bundled or downloaded; before it is ever run it is checked against the release SHA-256 digests shipped with the plugin or with `gh attestation verify` against hudsonwa/botport's release workflow, and its digest is pinned; `hermes twin setup --allow-unattested` is a recorded opt-out), plus `gh` (for that check only) and `hermes cron` (resume and pause on \"Move routines to Hermes\", create --paused when importing a setup export), each with a minimal environment; while the new-twins sheet watches for setup exports (at most 15 minutes, every 4 s) the plugin lists your Downloads folder (that folder only: names, dates, sizes) and reads new .json, .txt or .md files of at most 2 MB there and, with skill sync set up, in the shared folder's exports/ (nothing moved or deleted; only digests are kept); it writes the model you choose into each twin profile's config.yaml; via botport it reads the Grok desktop app's bot list and the selected bots' local transcript files (capped, parsed for routine changes only) and writes the twin Hermes profiles it manages; the plugin writes plugin-data/grok-bot-twin/, its own marked blocks in twin profiles' SOUL.md, plugins.enabled, a copy of itself and the allowlisted settings in each twin profile, a note per removed twin in profiles/.deleted/, and, only with their switches on, twins' skill files, memory files and cron jobs; botport keeps one owner-only key file per twin; it runs one background metadata-check/sync thread (default every 60 s) and one Grok-folder watch once a session starts, plus threads for the sharing features only while they are on; it sends no telemetry; it calls Hermes' internal skill scanner (tools.skills_guard, fail-open) for the demo's skill preview. An optional pre_tool_call hook is active only for 30 minutes after you start \"Let Hermes do the Grok part\" and tick that you approve each step: it makes Hermes ask before every computer_use step and refuses other apps, other chats and other typed text; pictures of the Grok window then go to that chat's model provider. Network: none unless you switch on sharing; then the computer with the Grok app listens on ONE private address (its Tailscale address, else its home-network address; never all interfaces or a public one; TCP 18777) and your linked computers connect only to that address; linking proves a one-time 12-character link code (SPEKE; the code never travels) and every later call and answer is signed with a per-computer key; plain HTTP, encrypted by Tailscale on Tailscale, readable but not forgeable on a plain home network, except a twin's memory, which the plugin encrypts end to end. Besides that, the plugin connects only to the model servers already set up in your default Hermes config.yaml, with one `GET <base_url>/models` each
