index d03222bd23..b187c78389 100644
--- a/plugin-catalog/secret-drop.yaml
+++ b/plugin-catalog/secret-drop.yaml
@@ -1,19 +1,17 @@
 name: secret-drop
 repo: https://github.com/Goitonthefloor/hermes-secret-drop
-sha: 505bd3b47937ae453d4a54a2ee2455a4d274bf42
-description: 'Store a secret on the gateway host without showing it to the model. Disclosure — stores the value in
-  plaintext in the profile .env (mode 0600) and adds the name to terminal.env_passthrough, so $NAME is available to the
-  terminal tool; the model can still read the value with its own tools (cat .env, echo $NAME). Keeps secrets out of
-  chat/prompt context and redacts verbatim output of credential-shaped values; it is not a boundary against a model
-  that deliberately reads it. Adds a static system-prompt section and a /secret command. Imports private core helpers
-  read-only (_config_profile_scope, _is_hermes_provider_credential, _CONFIG_LOCK).'
+sha: 3ca38b593bb11f88065387d5091b2c8ea31adc76
+description: "Give Hermes a secret without the value appearing in the chat. Disclosure — the value is stored in plaintext in the profile .env (mode 0600), and storing narrows the file to that mode itself instead of inheriting whatever mode it finds. The model references it as {{secret:NAME}}; a pre_tool_call hook substitutes the value in terminal and execute_code arguments in memory, immediately before the call runs, so the transcript, the prompt and the provider request carry the alias. The same hook guards the store as a whole: every string argument of every tool is inspected, including tools it does not know by name; write payloads are walked into nested edits; directory arguments such as workdir are resolved, and a persisted cwd into the profile home is refused; wildcard spellings are compared with the guarded file names; and a path is resolved before a name is matched, so a symlink alias does not slip through. A command that names the store, a bare environment dump, and a mutation aimed at the profile home are refused. Tool output is scrubbed of stored values, of credential-shaped environment entries and of credential values kept in the profile config file, in their common encodings. What it does not do: it is not a sandbox for the effects of a command the model writes, and the guards are denylists - a value that is short or word-like is stored but not searched for globally, and a program that reassembles a value is covered only by scrubbing of the values it knows. The desktop dialog and its nine locale bundles promise the chat, not model-inaccessibility. Adds a static system-prompt section, a /secret command and a desktop drop dialog. An optional username can be entered beside the value and is stored as NAME_USER; it is injectable through an alias but never used as a global output needle. Only a name this plugin wrote as a username is treated that way, tracked in an account index that belongs to the guarded store, so a secret the user stores directly under a NAME_USER name stays a credential whose value is scrubbed. Imports private core helpers read-only (_config_profile_scope, _is_hermes_provider_credential, _CONFIG_LOCK). On every session start it rewrites config.yaml to drop stored names from terminal.env_passthrough (migration from versions before 1.5.1), and it reads credential-shaped values from the profile config.yaml only to use them as output-scrub needles. A substituted value still leaves the host with whatever the command sends, and core's local tool-completion display receives the resolved arguments."
 maintainer: Goitonthefloor
 tier: community
 category: desktop
-version: "1.4.0"
+version: "1.6.5"
 capabilities:
   provides_tools: []
   provides_hooks:
     - on_session_start
+    - pre_tool_call
+    - transform_terminal_output
+    - transform_tool_result
   provides_middleware: []
   requires_env: []
